  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\n\nThe current algorithm's limitation is its inability to sufficiently explore the function space without being constrained to fixed grid sizes. We hypothesize that the optimal solution has multi-resolution properties. We'll leverage adaptive grid-size selection by doubling the resolution in each refinement stage, combined with a new coordinate descent heuristic that targets peak correlations directly. The key innovation is a three-phase progression that moves from coarse to extremely fine grids, ensuring shape preservation while improving the objective value. We'll prioritize minimum improvement conditions in each refinement to avoid stagnation and benefit from shape continuity when increasing grid size.\n\nMathematical specification:\n\nObjective: Minimize C_5(h) = max_k \u222b[0,2] h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\nSubject to:\n  0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x \u2208 [0,2]\n  \u222b[0,2] h(x) dx = 1\n\nRepresentation:\n  Discretize [0,2] into n= N * n_refine grid intervals\n  h(x_i) at x_i = i*dx (dx=2.0/n)\n  Maintained projection: \u2211h_i * dx = 1 \u2192 \u2211h_i = n/2\n\nOptimization:\n  Phase 1: Primal Nelder-Mead bayesian optimization with integral constraint (500s budget)\n  Phase 2: Local refinement with multi-start gradient projection (700s budget)\n  Phase 3: Resolution doubling (n \u2192 2n, 800s budget) with shape-preservation\n  Smoothed objective for gradient computation: \n      smooth_obj(\u03b1) = max_k cross_correlation(h) - \u03b1 * (n^2 - k_min)\n  Grid doubling uses piecewise linear interpolation\n\nImplementation procedure:\n\n1. Phase 1: Initial bayesian optimization over grid space (n=200)\n   - X = 200 grid points\n   - Initialize Gaussian process model of log(C_5)\n   - Use expected improvement acquisition\n   - 500 function evaluations\n   - Project feasible constraint: inject smoothing kernel before optimization\n\n2. Phase 2: Local refinement\n   - Convert best h to interpolation basis\n   - Apply gradient descent to minimize cross-correlation peaks\n   - Add 20 random restarts\n   - Iteratively prune inactive points\n\n3. Phase 3: Progressive grid doubling (n_next = n_refine * 2)\n   - For each doubling:\n       Linear interpolate grid points (preserve shape)\n       Generate perturbations at +/- 5% amplitude\n       Apply local optimization\n       Update constraint projection\n   - Stop when doubling exceeds budget\n\nSearch choices and useful variations:\n\n  Primary search: 500s phase 1 + 700s phase 2 + 800s phase 3\n  Secondary search parameters:\n      Initial grid: 200\n      Max grid size: 1600\u00d73 = 4800 points\n      Perturbation schedule: decreasing amplitude factor (0.95 every doubling)\n      Feature: enable/disable interferometric peaks detection during phase 2\n\n  Adaptive resolution doubling when:\n      (a) Budget remaining > 1min\n      (b) Current C_5 not improving for 3 consecutive phases\n\nComputational budget and robustness:\n\n  Dominant costs:\n    Bayesian phase: O(n log n) per evaluation\n    Grid doubling: 3\u00d7 resolution factor\n    Memory: O(n_eff^2) with n_eff= documenting\n\n  Time checking points:\n    Phase 1: budget split at 50 evaluations\n    Phase 2: split at 50% improved_instances\n    Phase 3: per-doubling wall-time limits\n\n  Fallback strategy:\n    If grid doubling fails: select best point from current grid interpolation\n    If no allocation: keep previous solution\n\nValidation and return contract:\n\n  Must ensure:\n    1. 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i\n    2. \u2211 h_i \u2248 n/2 (tolerance=1e-6)\n    3. C_5 < current best\n\n  Post-processing:\n    Recompute C_5 with mode=\"full\"\n    Return tuple (h_final, computed_C_5, final_n)\n\n  Shape preservation conditions:\n    Linear interpolation between grids\n    Gradient projection maintains constraints\n    If derivation exceeds time, use conservative fallback\n\nThis implementation requires careful timing management during grid doubling phases and adaptive budget allocation across search phases. The bayesian optimization phase requires full length kernel calculation which should be optimized with lazy evaluation. The interpolation technique should preserve differentiable structure, implying that cross-phase grid combinations must be mathematically consistent.",
